WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.560
This bottle of estuary water looks just like

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:05.960
tap water, but it's carrying dissolved salt.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:07.880
How much chloride is in it?

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:10.960
You already know how a titration counts acid,

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.680
with a burette, a pipette and n equals

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:14.640
c V.

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:17.600
Chloride isn't an acid or a base, though,

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:19.760
so a p H indicator has nothing to

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:20.560
respond to.

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:22.880
So how do we see the moment the

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:24.160
chloride runs out?

00:00:25.367 --> 00:00:28.446
The trick is to titrate with silver nitrate.

00:00:28.727 --> 00:00:31.846
Silver ions grab chloride ions and form silver

00:00:31.927 --> 00:00:33.686
chloride, a white solid.

00:00:34.247 --> 00:00:37.126
One silver for one chloride, so the ratio

00:00:37.287 --> 00:00:38.166
is one to one.

00:00:38.807 --> 00:00:41.286
As the silver nitrate runs in, the flask

00:00:41.367 --> 00:00:42.846
turns cloudy white.

00:00:43.127 --> 00:00:44.246
But here's the problem.

00:00:44.647 --> 00:00:46.726
The white keeps forming right up to the

00:00:46.807 --> 00:00:49.446
end, and then it simply stops forming.

00:00:49.927 --> 00:00:52.086
Nothing new appears to tell you to stop.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.300
So we add a few drops of potassium

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:57.020
chromate before we start.

00:00:57.220 --> 00:00:59.660
It's yellow, and it's our indicator.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:02.660
While chloride is still around, the silver keeps

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:04.740
forming white silver chloride.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:07.300
Once nearly all the chloride is gone, the

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:10.340
next silver ions meet chromate instead, and form

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.300
silver chromate, a red-brown solid.

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:15.620
Two silvers for each chromate.

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:18.580
The first faint red-brown tinge that stays after

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:20.580
swirling is the endpoint.

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:23.140
In this titration, the signal is a second

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:24.020
precipitate.

00:01:25.234 --> 00:01:27.473
Why does the chloride react first?

00:01:28.034 --> 00:01:30.673
You'll sometimes read that silver chloride is less

00:01:30.754 --> 00:01:32.713
soluble, so it goes first.

00:01:32.994 --> 00:01:35.393
Careful, that isn't the real reason.

00:01:35.874 --> 00:01:37.313
Think back to Q s p.

00:01:37.794 --> 00:01:40.593
A solid only forms once its ion product

00:01:40.674 --> 00:01:41.953
passes K s p.

00:01:42.594 --> 00:01:45.073
At the start there's lots of chloride, about

00:01:45.154 --> 00:01:48.273
point zero seven four molar, so silver chloride

00:01:48.354 --> 00:01:50.513
needs only a tiny trace of silver.

00:01:51.074 --> 00:01:53.553
There's only a little chromate, about point zero

00:01:53.714 --> 00:01:56.513
zero five molar, and silver is squared in

00:01:56.594 --> 00:01:59.153
its K s p, so silver chromate needs

00:01:59.314 --> 00:02:00.993
thousands of times more silver.

00:02:01.714 --> 00:02:04.033
As the chloride gets used up, the silver

00:02:04.114 --> 00:02:06.353
needed for silver chloride climbs.

00:02:06.834 --> 00:02:09.153
The red only appears when more than ninety

00:02:09.314 --> 00:02:11.233
nine point nine percent of the chloride is

00:02:11.394 --> 00:02:11.793
gone.

00:02:12.274 --> 00:02:14.513
That's why only a small, set amount of

00:02:14.594 --> 00:02:15.793
chromate goes in.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:20.040
This endpoint works only in the right conditions.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:23.240
Keep the solution near neutral, roughly p H

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:24.680
six and a half to ten.

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:28.360
In acid, chromate ions pick up hydrogen ions

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:31.400
and turn into dichromate, so there's less chromate

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:32.280
to make the red.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:35.160
The endpoint comes late, and the titre reads

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:35.640
high.

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:39.560
In strong base, silver forms brown silver oxide

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:40.200
instead.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:43.720
Other ions that precipitate with silver, like bromide

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:45.960
or iodide, get counted as if they were

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:46.600
chloride.

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:49.000
And the red needs a tiny excess of

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:51.480
silver before you can see it, so chemists

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:53.480
often run a blank and subtract it.

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:55.700
One more thing.

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:59.980
Potassium chromate contains chromium six, which is toxic

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:03.260
and treated as a cancer-causing chemical, so many

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:05.340
schools skip this practical.

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:07.100
You're more likely to meet the Mohr method

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:09.153
as data in a question.

00:03:09.287 --> 00:03:10.540
So let's process some.

00:03:11.767 --> 00:03:14.246
A twenty five millilitre aliquot of the estuary

00:03:14.327 --> 00:03:17.526
water is titrated with point one zero zero

00:03:17.647 --> 00:03:19.646
zero molar silver nitrate.

00:03:19.927 --> 00:03:21.126
Here are the titres.

00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:23.526
The rough run comes first and gets left

00:03:23.607 --> 00:03:24.006
out.

00:03:24.407 --> 00:03:26.726
The other three agree within point one zero

00:03:26.887 --> 00:03:29.206
millilitres, so they're concordant.

00:03:29.447 --> 00:03:32.886
Their average is eighteen point five zero millilitres.

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:38.180
First, the task: find the chloride concentration, in

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:40.500
moles per litre and grams per litre.

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:45.140
The equation: silver plus chloride gives silver chloride.

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:47.780
Start with the solution you know both numbers

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:49.460
for, the silver nitrate.

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:52.020
Moles equals c times V.

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:55.540
Point one zero zero zero, times point zero

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:58.660
one eight five zero litres, gives one point

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:00.980
eight five zero times ten to the minus

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:02.660
three moles of silver.

00:04:03.867 --> 00:04:06.586
The ratio is one to one, so that's

00:04:06.667 --> 00:04:09.306
also the moles of chloride, and all of

00:04:09.387 --> 00:04:11.066
it came from the aliquot.

00:04:11.187 --> 00:04:14.266
So divide by the aliquot's volume, point zero

00:04:14.427 --> 00:04:17.346
two five zero zero litres, not by the

00:04:17.387 --> 00:04:18.346
titre.

00:04:18.427 --> 00:04:21.226
That gives point zero seven four zero moles

00:04:21.307 --> 00:04:22.106
per litre.

00:04:23.334 --> 00:04:26.133
For grams per litre, multiply by the molar

00:04:26.214 --> 00:04:29.653
mass of chlorine, thirty five point four five.

00:04:29.694 --> 00:04:31.733
That's about two point six two grams of

00:04:31.814 --> 00:04:33.573
chloride per litre.

00:04:33.654 --> 00:04:36.693
Seawater has roughly nineteen, so this water is

00:04:36.814 --> 00:04:39.493
only lightly salty, what you'd expect where a

00:04:39.574 --> 00:04:40.773
river meets the sea.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:43.440
So here's the short version.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:46.560
White while chloride lasts, and stop at the

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:48.400
first red-brown that stays.

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:50.000
Keep it near neutral.

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:53.680
Then titre, moles, ratio, and divide by the

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:54.320
aliquot.

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:56.800
And the one to one ratio is silver

00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:57.680
to chloride.

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:00.160
The two to one belongs to chromate, the

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:02.480
indicator, so leave it out of the answer.

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:04.660
Your turn.

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:07.860
Bore water is tested by the Mohr method.

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:11.060
Find the chloride concentration in moles per litre

00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:13.300
and in grams per litre.

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:15.060
Pause here to answer.

00:05:25.467 --> 00:05:27.706
Leave out the rough titre and average the

00:05:27.907 --> 00:05:31.186
rest: fourteen point six six millilitres.

00:05:31.467 --> 00:05:33.546
That gives the moles of silver, and the

00:05:33.627 --> 00:05:35.146
same moles of chloride.

00:05:35.387 --> 00:05:38.586
Divide by the twenty millilitre sample: point zero

00:05:38.667 --> 00:05:40.106
one eight three molar.

00:05:40.427 --> 00:05:43.306
Times thirty five point four five, that's point

00:05:43.387 --> 00:05:45.666
six five zero grams per litre.

00:05:45.947 --> 00:05:47.946
Doubling the moles with chromate's two to one

00:05:48.027 --> 00:05:50.026
ratio is the classic slip.

00:05:51.234 --> 00:05:51.873
Last one.

00:05:52.514 --> 00:05:54.913
Wastewater from a pickling factory has a p

00:05:54.994 --> 00:05:57.713
H of three, and a student titrates it

00:05:57.794 --> 00:06:00.033
by the Mohr method without adjusting the p

00:06:00.194 --> 00:06:00.833
H.

00:06:01.154 --> 00:06:04.593
Explain the effect on the calculated chloride concentration,

00:06:04.674 --> 00:06:06.033
and suggest a fix.

00:06:06.354 --> 00:06:07.713
Pause here to answer.

00:06:18.134 --> 00:06:21.173
At p H three, hydrogen ions turn much

00:06:21.254 --> 00:06:23.413
of the chromate into dichromate.

00:06:23.654 --> 00:06:26.533
With less chromate, extra silver is needed before

00:06:26.614 --> 00:06:29.813
red-brown silver chromate shows, so the titre's too

00:06:29.974 --> 00:06:32.693
high, and so is the calculated chloride.

00:06:33.254 --> 00:06:36.013
Fix it by neutralising the sample first, for

00:06:36.054 --> 00:06:39.173
example with sodium hydrogen carbonate, to about p

00:06:39.254 --> 00:06:40.373
H seven or eight.

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:44.720
So silver counts the chloride, and chromate tells

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:45.680
you when to stop.

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:49.600
Next: instead of measuring a volume, we'll weigh

00:06:49.640 --> 00:06:51.120
the precipitate itself.

00:06:52.334 --> 00:06:53.773
Here are the key notes.

00:06:54.014 --> 00:06:55.933
Pause the video if you need more time

00:06:56.014 --> 00:06:56.813
to copy them.
